  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through next Friday)\nIssued at 312 PM EDT Sat Jun 13 2026\n\nThe surface high looks to retreat some to the east, this comes in\nresponse to a trough swinging across the Southeast United States.\nThe result of this will be that winds will become more southerly\nto south-southeast. With precipitable water values remaining in\nexcess of 2.2 inches, especially across the east coast, diurnal\nshowers and storms will remain the forecast during this time. The\nprimary focus of this will be more in the eastern portions of the\nforecast area through much of the week before transitioning back\nto a more interior convective zone later in the week as the\nsurface high expands back to the west and winds become more\neasterly to southeasterly. The main impacts with these storms will\nbe from locally heavy downpours that could lead to urban and poor\ndrainage flooding, cloud to ground lightning, strong gusty winds. \n\nTemperatures will be above normal through the period, with heat\nindices also keeping around 100-105 degrees. There are some\nlocations looking to be 105-110 degrees later in the week,\nespecially across the southwest Peninsula. This raises the\nHeatRisk in some of these areas to the major category, so\ndefinitely a time for folks to pay attention if they will be\nspending time outside.",
